  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 210 AM MDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nThe cold front did not disappoint in bringing some relief from \nthe heat on Tuesday. We will see one more day of sub 90s today \nbefore the upper level ridge pushes slightly east, centering \nitself over the Four Corners and Great Basin for the rest of the \nweek. This all-too-familiar pattern will be responsible for\nanother hot and dry week, with temperatures still expected to \ngradually warm each day, with the urban corridor reaching the mid\nto upper 90s by Thursday. \n\nThe main concern for today will be with increasing northwesterly \nflow bringing gusty winds to the higher elevations where very low \nrelative humidity will be in place (single digits). A Red Flag \nWarning is in effect for the northern mountains and high valleys \nfrom 12 PM to 7 PM today. Across the lower elevations, early \nmorning fog and low clouds will help keep cooler temperatures (mid\nto upper 70s) in place across the far eastern plains, with mid to\nupper 80s expected for the urban corridor. Northerly flow from an\nearly morning surface cyclone is expected to bring some low clouds\nand potentially some fog into the urban corridor near sunrise.\nThis is expected to clear out through the morning, but this could\nkeep temperatures a few degrees cooler than forecast if it sticks\naround longer than expected. While smoke will remain across the \narea today, conditions should improve slightly compared to\nyesterday before another surge of heavier concentrations arrives \nby Wednesday morning. \n\nPrecipitation chances continue to look bleak under the persistent\nridge, with dry conditions expected through most of the forecast\nperiod. The next opportunity for measurable precipitation arrives\nSunday afternoon as PWATs increase to near-normal values over the\nmountains. Even then, precipitation amounts are expected to \nremain light, with only a few hundredths possible for the \nmountains from I-70 southward.",
